WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.909
Every cleaning crew keeps a logbook.

2
00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:15.120
After each round, someone writes one line: when it happened, what kind of round it was and why, how full the floor was before and after, and how long the workers waited.

3
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:19.455
After a bad afternoon, that logbook is how you find out what happened.

4
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.516
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:23.516 --> 00:00:25.854
My requests got slow this afternoon.

6
00:00:25.854 --> 00:00:27.738
Was it the garbage collector?

7
00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:29.427
And how would I even know?

8
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.682
The JVM keeps that logbook too, if you ask.

9
00:00:33.682 --> 00:00:39.940
One flag, -Xlog:gc, makes the garbage collector write a line for every collection.

10
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.137
Older guides show PrintGCDetails, which JDK 9 replaced.

11
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:53.320
Here's the first line from G1, the default collector, on JDK 25, running the thumbnail service's stand-in load.

12
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.808
It looks like noise.

13
00:00:54.808 --> 00:00:55.477
It isn't.

14
00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:59.195
Let's read it left to right, like a logbook entry.

15
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:04.886
0.440s, in brackets, is when: the time since the JVM started.

16
00:01:04.886 --> 00:01:10.803
Then the log level, info, and the tag, gc, the topic of the line.

17
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.357
GC(0) numbers the collection.

18
00:01:13.357 --> 00:01:15.877
Round zero, the very first one.

19
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.963
Pause Young is the event, the kind of round.

20
00:01:18.963 --> 00:01:22.188
Pause means every application thread was stopped.

21
00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:25.809
Normal says it cleaned only the young part of the heap.

22
00:01:25.809 --> 00:01:30.877
And G1 Evacuation Pause is the why: eden, the area for new objects, was full.

23
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.294
Then, how full the floor was.

24
00:01:33.294 --> 00:01:36.365
28M, an arrow, 2M, and 214M in brackets.

25
00:01:36.365 --> 00:01:47.577
That's the heap in use before the pause, the heap in use after it, and the committed heap, the memory the operating system has really handed over.

26
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:52.622
And last, 8.692ms: how long every one of your threads stood still.

27
00:01:52.622 --> 00:01:56.621
That's the number a person waiting on a request feels.

28
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:02.478
Now a stretch from the same run, with the start of each line cut off.

29
00:02:02.478 --> 00:02:03.268
Seven lines.

30
00:02:03.268 --> 00:02:05.376
Which ones stopped your threads?

31
00:02:05.376 --> 00:02:06.429
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:14.060
The tempting answer is every line that ends in milliseconds.

33
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.722
But look at the second Concurrent Mark Cycle line.

34
00:02:17.722 --> 00:02:24.385
That cycle took 25.055 ms, and it ran beside your program, while your threads kept working.

35
00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:29.092
So here's the rule, and it works for every HotSpot collector.

36
00:02:29.092 --> 00:02:35.445
A pause line has Pause right after GC and its number, and ends with a duration in ms.

37
00:02:35.445 --> 00:02:41.124
Five lines pass: Concurrent Start, Remark, Cleanup, Prepare Mixed and Mixed.

38
00:02:41.124 --> 00:02:42.245
26.5 ms in all.

39
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:44.537
It's like a bread recipe.

40
00:02:44.537 --> 00:02:47.936
Knead for five minutes, then let it rise for an hour.

41
00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:50.501
Only the kneading keeps your hands busy.

42
00:02:50.501 --> 00:02:55.504
So the point is: read the word right after GC, not just the number at the end.

43
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.553
Now look at the last two lines: Evacuation Failure, twice in a row.

44
00:03:01.553 --> 00:03:07.937
G1 ran out of free regions to copy into, so this heap is too small for what's live.

45
00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:13.014
JDK 21 writes just Evacuation Failure, and JDK 25 adds the reason.

46
00:03:13.014 --> 00:03:17.013
Search for Evacuation Failure, and you'll find both.

47
00:03:17.933 --> 00:03:24.904
-Xlog:gc with a star turns on every GC tag: phases, region counts, metaspace and CPU time.

48
00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:30.171
That's ten to fifteen lines per pause, for when you need the detail.

49
00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:34.887
For a service, write the log to rotating files with the time of day.

50
00:03:34.887 --> 00:03:37.299
The colons split it into four parts.

51
00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:45.201
What to log, where to write it, the decorations at the start of each line, and the rotation: at most 5 files of 20 MB.

52
00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:49.492
Here a paper logbook and the JVM part ways.

53
00:03:49.492 --> 00:03:58.084
When the current gc.log is full, the JVM renames it to a numbered file, like gc.log.0, and starts a fresh one.

54
00:03:58.084 --> 00:04:02.068
And with five files, the oldest one is thrown away.

55
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:10.432
The lesson's demo does just this, with tiny 40 KB files so you can watch them rotate.

56
00:04:10.432 --> 00:04:13.667
Two seconds of load in a 256 MB heap.

57
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:17.339
The first young pause went from 29M to 2M.

58
00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:23.983
The last ones left 120 to 245 MB behind: the floor rose as the cache filled.

59
00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:31.065
And the files held 2 Pause Full lines, and 7 to 13 lines with Evacuation Failure.

60
00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:34.120
So leave the logbook on in production.

61
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.243
It costs a few lines per collection, and in an incident it's the first thing you'll want.

62
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.318
A logbook you start after the bad afternoon is just a diary of the next one.

63
00:04:44.318 --> 00:04:46.453
So turn it on before you need it.

64
00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:49.363
So, was it the garbage collector?

65
00:04:49.363 --> 00:04:50.408
Open the logbook.

66
00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:56.435
Find the lines with Pause right after the number, and read how long each one stopped your threads.

67
00:04:56.435 --> 00:04:58.157
Then watch the after-values.

68
00:04:58.157 --> 00:05:01.724
If that floor keeps rising, something is filling the heap.

69
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:03.906
Quick check.

70
00:05:03.906 --> 00:05:08.040
GC(17) Concurrent Mark Cycle 25.055ms.

71
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.372
Did your threads stand still for 25 milliseconds?

72
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:16.794
No.

73
00:05:16.794 --> 00:05:21.523
There's no Pause after the number, so that cycle ran beside your threads.

74
00:05:21.523 --> 00:05:25.409
Its two pauses, Remark and Cleanup, have lines of their own.
